  "narrative": {
    "top_frame": "They never led. St. Louis broke through in the fifth, and the Giants' offense couldn't answer. Twelve strikeouts and one run on six hits left them chasing a game they never caught.",
    "what_this_means": "One run on six hits is not a slump. It's a locked door. They had nine plate appearances with runners in scoring position and converted exactly one into a run. Jonah Cox drove in that single run, but the rest of the order failed when it mattered. The critical chances came in the seventh and ninth innings, with runners stranded both times. Roupp lasted 4.1 innings and gave up just two earned runs; the bullpen. Kuhnel, Harris, and Seymour combining for 3.2 scoreless innings. Held the line. The pitching was good enough to win. The offense was not. This is how inconsistency looks: the bats go silent even when the pitching buys them time. They're now 29.5 games back with a four-game losing streak and a .714 OPS. That margin only gets wider when they leave runners on base in bunches.",
    "what_to_watch": "Pallante carries a 3.45 ERA into tonight's rematch at Busch Stadium. Blade Tidwell gets the ball for the Giants at 4:45 PM PT. Another chance to prove the bullpen hold from last night was not wasted.",
